{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.app.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    /*
     * TIER 1 — the near-term target (WEB-CI-007).
     *
     * These are the checks this migration is actually about: null safety and
     * implicit any. Together they currently produce ~88 errors, which is a
     * tractable number to ratchet down.
     */
    "strict": true,
    "noImplicitAny": true,
    "strictNullChecks": true,
    "strictFunctionTypes": true,
    "strictBindCallApply": true,
    "strictPropertyInitialization": true,
    "noImplicitThis": true,
    "alwaysStrict": true,
    "noImplicitReturns": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,

    /* Import Checks */
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true

    /*
     * TIER 2 — deliberately DEFERRED, not forgotten.
     *
     *   "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true
     *   "noPropertyAccessFromIndexSignature": true
     *   "noUnusedLocals": true
     *   "noUnusedParameters": true
     *
     * These were enabled here previously and accounted for 185 of the 273 errors
     * on their own. 78 were TS4111 alone, which only asks that `obj.foo` be
     * written `obj['foo']` on index-signature types. That volume of mechanical
     * noise buried the genuine null-safety failures and made the gate
     * unactionable, which is a large part of why this migration never moved.
     *
     * Re-enable one at a time once tier 1 reaches zero, each as its own ratchet
     * step. noUnusedLocals/noUnusedParameters are cheapest and should go first;
     * ESLint already reports most of what they would.
     */
  },
  /*
   * NOTE ON HOW THIS LIST ACTUALLY BEHAVES (WEB-CI-007).
   *
   * `include` does NOT limit what TypeScript checks. tsc checks every file
   * reachable by import from these roots, so this project already reports errors
   * across ~59 files even though only nine roots were listed. Adding a "batch of
   * compliant files" therefore does not tighten the gate the way it appears to —
   * it adds roots, which usually pulls MORE non-compliant files into the graph
   * and makes the count go up.
   *
   * That is why enforcement lives in scripts/strict-ratchet.mjs — a per-file
   * error baseline that must not regress — rather than in this list. Grow this
   * list when a subtree is genuinely clean; use the ratchet to hold the line
   * everywhere else.
   */
  "include": [
    "src/vite-env.d.ts",
    "src/lib/**/*.ts",
    "src/contexts/AuthContext.tsx",
    "src/hooks/useEvents.ts",
    "src/hooks/useRestaurants.ts",
    "src/hooks/useAttractions.ts",
    "src/hooks/useSubscription.ts",
    "src/hooks/useTripPlanner.ts",
    "src/hooks/useAuth.ts",
    "src/hooks/useEventBySlug.ts",
    "src/hooks/useActiveAds.ts"
  ],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "dist", "build"]
}
